    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\n1970 Plymouth Road Runner Packs the Holy Trinity: 426 Hemi, 4-Speed, for Sale, and It's a Lot\n20 photos\nPhoto: REV! Muscle Cars\nWhen Plymouth introduced the Road Runner for the 1968 model year, the mission was simple: strip away luxury fluff, drop in big-block Mopar muscle, and undercut every rival on price. Over half a century later, top-tier examples command serious collector money, and this 1970 Plymouth Road Runner 426 Hemi 4-speed hardtop proves why.\nDocumented with its original fender tag, factory broadcast sheets, and numbers-matching drivetrain, it represents classic Chrysler street warfare at its sharpest.\nPlymouth built the Road Runner across 13 model years from 1968 through 1980, spanning three distinct B-body generations before transitioning to a compact trim package. The first generation ran from 1968 to 1970 on a 116-inch wheelbase, earning Motor Trend Car of the Year honors in 1969. A fuselage-styled second generation followed from 1971 to 1974 on a 115-inch platform, preceding a one-year 1975 B-body third generation and the 1976–1980 Plymouth Volaré era.\nBody styles evolved dramatically as safety rules, insurance surcharges, and buyer tastes reshaped Detroit during the 1970s. Early buyers between 1968 and 1970 could order a two-door pillared coupe, a two-door hardtop, or—for 1969 and 1970 only—a two-door convertible, alongside the aerodynamic (but questionably looking) 1970 Superbird hardtop.\nPhoto: REV! Muscle Cars From 1971 through 1974, Plymouth restricted the Road Runner strictly to a two-door hardtop, before offering a two-door pillared coupe in 1975 and compact Volaré coupes afterward.\nEngine choices defined the bird's reputation throughout its run. First-generation cars came standard with a 335-hp/425-lb-ft 383-cubic-inch four-barrel V8, while buyers could step up to the 390-hp 440 Six-Barrel or the 425-hp 426-cubic-inch Hemi. Subsequent generations introduced the 340, 400, 318, and 360 V8s—and even a 225-cubic-inch Slant-Six in 1979—mated across various years to 3-speed manual, 4-speed manual, 4-speed overdrive, or 3-speed TorqueFlite automatic transmissions.\nBy 1970, insurance premiums, and competition from Plymouth's own Duster and the restyled E-body Barracuda trimmed standard Road Runner production to 41,484 units worldwide (36,861 U.S. deliveries), plus at least 1,935 Superbirds. Hardtops led the tally at 24,944 cars, followed by 15,716 pillared coupes and 824 convertibles. Under the hood, 39,517 left the factory with the 383 V8, 1,815 carried the 440 Six-Barrel (introduced mid-year), and just 152 packed the 426 Hemi.\nPhoto: REV! Muscle Cars Within that 152-car Hemi run, Plymouth assembled 74 coupes, 3 convertibles, and 75 hardtops, splitting the hardtops into 59 examples with 4-speed manuals and 16 TorqueFlite automatics. While 1970 buyers could choose a 3-speed manual, 4-speed manual, or 3-speed automatic behind lesser engines, checking the E74 Hemi box added $871.45 to a $3,034 hardtop base price. Equipped with the A33 Track Pak and N96 Air Grabber, a hemi Road Runner could easily top $4,550.\nFor 1970, the Road Runner Hemi had lost a lot of its original touch. Back in ’68, when the moniker made landfall, nearly half of the 2,289 Hemi engines were dropped in a Road Runner (1,019 units). Just two years later, the percentage had fallen to about one in ten (152 out of a grand total of 1,543 Plymouths and Dodges armed with the mighty 426-cuber).\nThat original investment explains why surviving 4-speed Hemi hardtops sit near the top of the Mopar hierarchy. This St. Louis-built example left the line on January 20, 1970, and stayed with one family for over 30 years. It was restored once in their care, then it later received a thorough mechanical and cosmetic refresh before entering a private collection, showing 59,195 miles today.\nPhoto: REV! Muscle Cars Finished in factory EB5 Blue Fire Metallic across both its lower body and slick-top roof, the muscle car wears a purposeful sleeper specification. Whoever checked the order sheet skipped the optional hood blackout and fuselage dust trails, leaving only a white V8W transverse tail stripe, Road Runner fender decals, and subtle “Hemi” cowl badges to hint at its firepower. Quad headlights, a waterfall grille, J45 hood pins, and the N96 Air Grabber scoop complete the mugshot of this big-bore leviathan.\nInside, the cabin pairs Black Velvet upper door frames with charcoal-and-black premium vinyl bucket seats in crisp, blemish-free condition . A woodgrain center console frames the unmistakable Hurst pistol-grip shifter connected to the 4-speed cogbox. Full factory gauges include the Tic-Toc-Tach, complemented by an Music Master 2-watt AM radio and a woodgrain steering wheel with its working Road Runner horn button.\nBeneath the Air Grabber hood sits the original 426 Hemi V8, breathing through dual Carter 4-barrel carburetors for 425 hp and 490 lb-ft of torque. Mopar authority Frank Badalson verified both the oil-pan rail VIN stamp and final assembly stamp as factory-authentic. OE Gold Award winner Steve Bean pulled and rebuilt the engine, K-member, and front suspension to concours standards, coating the block in Hemi Orange while preserving original pan casting runs.\nPhoto: REV! Muscle Cars Factory Track Pak hardware makes this hardtop far more than a straight-line showpiece. The package bundled a 9¾-inch Dana 60 rear axle with 3.54:1 Sure-Grip gears, Hemi-spec suspension, a 7-blade fan, and a 26-inch radiator, backed by B51 power front disc brakes. Underneath, rust-free frame rails and floor pans retain factory undercoating alongside rebuilt steering linkage, refinished torsion bars, resurfaced original rotors, balanced driveshaft, fresh shocks, and correct exhaust.\nSerious Mopar collectors prize provenance almost as much as cubic inches, and this Road Runner backs up its hardware with an airtight paper trail. The original fender tag—complete with its matching 181 plant inspection stamp—and factory broadcast sheets reside in a document safe, accompanied by inspection reports and stamp photography.\nBetween its 1-of-59 drivetrain combination, long-term single-family history, and concours-fresh mechanicals, very few 1970 B-bodies check as many boxes. For enthusiasts hunting an investment-grade 4-speed Hemi Mopar with verified numbers and documented history, the wait might be over.\nThis 1970 Plymouth Road Runner is currently offered for sale by Rev Muscle Cars in Lake Villa, Illinois, carrying an asking price of $295,000. That puts it squarely in blue-chip collector territory, yet right in line with what top-flight, concours-detailed 1970 Hemi 4-speed hardtops command on today's market.\nVia // REV! Muscle Cars\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHemi Mopar Plymouth Road Runner muscle car 1970 Plymouth Road Runner Mopar Road Runner Hemi Plymouth\nAbout the author: Razvan Calin\nAfter nearly two decades in news television, Răzvan turned to a different medium. He’s been a field journalist, a TV producer, and a seafarer but found that he feels right at home among petrolheads. Full profile\nUP NEXT Six Ghost Supercars That almost Reached Production Before Someone Pulled the Plug",
